## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical risk management when relying on external data and requires a concrete rollout plan across regions with resilience patterns and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and canary deployments
- SLOs and data freshness metrics
- Resilience patterns: circuit breakers, fallback caches
- Rollout artifacts, runbooks, and incident drills

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight circuit-breaker with fallback
class Breaker {
  constructor(threshold=3) { this.failures=0; this.open=false; }
  async call(fn) {
    if (this.open) throw new Error('Circuit open');
    try { return await fn(); } catch (e) { this.failures++; if (this.failures >= threshold) this.open=true; throw e; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ETA accuracy during canary without impacting users?
- How would you design post-incident reviews and remedial actions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to translate reliability risks into concrete actions, balancing external vendor risk with internal velocity, and creating observable gates and rituals.

## Key Concepts

- Reliability budgets and SLO/SLI definitions
- Backpressure and circuit breakers in distributed systems
- Chaos testing and staged rollout plans
- Incident management and post-incident reviews

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: calculate if burn rate exceeds budget
function burnRate(slo, breaches, windowHours){
  const budget = 1 - slo;
  const spend = breaches / windowHours;
  return spend / budget;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust SLOs if the partner outage lasts longer than a day?
- What dashboards and alerts would you use to prevent reactive firefighting?
